From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CatPieLeaf <catpieleaf@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] USB: fix two ASIX AX88179/178A gigabit hangs

The ASIX AX88179 USB 3.0 Gigabit Ethernet chip (0b95:1790) wedges
under sustained wired traffic in two distinct, unrelated ways.

Part 1 (drivers/usb/core/quirks.c): the harsher failure. The driver
logs a spontaneous

  ax88179_178a 2-2:1.0 enp5s0f3u2: ax88179 - Link status is: 0

with no cable pull, and the interface never comes back. The xHC port
it is on subsequently fails to re-enable it:

  usb usb3-port4: Cannot enable. Maybe the USB cable is bad?
  usb usb3-port4: attempt power cycle
  usb usb3-port4: unable to enumerate USB device

The device only recovers once something forces a real bus/port reset
(e.g. `usbreset 0b95:1790`, or physically unplugging it), which
re-enumerates it from scratch and restores link.

This matches the same USB3 LPM (U1/U2) interaction that Realtek's
RTL8153-based gigabit dongles hit, already worked around via
USB_QUIRK_NO_LPM on 0bda:8153: SuperSpeed gigabit ethernet chips
autonegotiating link power states appear to fail to come back out of
U1/U2 during real traffic bursts and wedge until externally reset.
Disable LPM for the AX88179/178A the same way to keep it out of that
state.

Part 2 (drivers/net/usb/ax88179_178a.c): a separate, softer failure
that the LPM quirk above does not address. The AX88179/178A driver
never implements driver_info->recover, the hook usbnet_tx_timeout()
calls (in addition to unlinking pending TX URBs) when the net_device
TX watchdog fires:

  void usbnet_tx_timeout(struct net_device *net, unsigned int txqueue)
  {
          ...
          unlink_urbs(dev, &dev->txq);
          queue_work(system_bh_wq, &dev->bh_work);
          if (dev->driver_info->recover)
                  (dev->driver_info->recover)(dev);
  }

With no .recover, a watchdog timeout only unlinks the stuck URBs and
reschedules the usbnet worker - it never actually pokes the hardware.
If the chip's USB/PHY state is genuinely wedged (as opposed to a
transient queue backup), nothing brings it back: the interface stays
"up" (carrier and link state untouched, since this path never touches
them) while no traffic passes - again only recovered by a real
bus-level `usbreset`, and only until it wedges again, since nothing
in the kernel ever performs that reset automatically. This is why
disabling LPM alone was not sufficient to fix connectivity loss under
sustained traffic.

drivers/net/usb/cdc_subset.c's ALi M5632 minidriver already hits the
same class of problem and defines exactly this recovery shape:

  static void m5632_recover(struct usbnet *dev)
  {
          struct usb_device *udev = dev->udev;
          struct usb_interface *intf = dev->intf;
          int r;

          r = usb_lock_device_for_reset(udev, intf);
          if (r < 0)
                  return;
          usb_reset_device(udev);
          usb_unlock_device(udev);
  }

Add the same pattern here - the in-kernel equivalent of the userspace
`usbreset` workaround - and wire it up for both AX88179 and AX88178A,
the two device IDs backed by this file (0b95:1790 / 0b95:178a).

Reported by RakuOS (a downstream Linux distribution) users running
its kernel-p03 build.

Signed-off-by: CatPieLeaf <catpieleaf@proton.me>
---
 drivers/net/usb/ax88179_178a.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 drivers/usb/core/quirks.c      |  3 +++
 2 files changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/usb/ax88179_178a.c b/drivers/net/usb/ax88179_178a.c
--- a/drivers/net/usb/ax88179_178a.c
+++ b/drivers/net/usb/ax88179_178a.c
@@ -1707,6 +1707,19 @@ static int ax88179_stop(struct usbnet *dev)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static void ax88179_recover(struct usbnet *dev)
+{
+	struct usb_device *udev = dev->udev;
+	struct usb_interface *intf = dev->intf;
+	int r;
+
+	r = usb_lock_device_for_reset(udev, intf);
+	if (r < 0)
+		return;
+	usb_reset_device(udev);
+	usb_unlock_device(udev);
+}
+
 static const struct driver_info ax88179_info = {
 	.description = "ASIX AX88179 USB 3.0 Gigabit Ethernet",
 	.bind = ax88179_bind,
@@ -1714,6 +1727,7 @@ static const struct driver_info ax88179_info = {
 	.status = ax88179_status,
 	.link_reset = ax88179_link_reset,
 	.reset = ax88179_net_reset,
+	.recover = ax88179_recover,
 	.stop = ax88179_stop,
 	.flags = FLAG_ETHER | FLAG_FRAMING_AX,
 	.rx_fixup = ax88179_rx_fixup,
@@ -1725,6 +1739,7 @@ static const struct driver_info ax88178a_info = {
 	.status = ax88179_status,
 	.link_reset = ax88179_link_reset,
 	.reset = ax88179_net_reset,
+	.recover = ax88179_recover,
 	.stop = ax88179_stop,
 	.flags = FLAG_ETHER | FLAG_FRAMING_AX,
 	.rx_fixup = ax88179_rx_fixup,
diff --git a/drivers/usb/core/quirks.c b/drivers/usb/core/quirks.c
--- a/drivers/usb/core/quirks.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/core/quirks.c
@@ -457,6 +457,9 @@ static const struct usb_device_id usb_quirk_list[] = {
 	/* ASUS TUF 4K PRO - BOS descriptor fetch hangs at SuperSpeed Plus */
 	{ USB_DEVICE(0x0b05, 0x1ab9), .driver_info = USB_QUIRK_NO_BOS },
 
+	/* ASIX AX88179/178A USB 3.0 Gigabit Ethernet - wedges under LPM */
+	{ USB_DEVICE(0x0b95, 0x1790), .driver_info = USB_QUIRK_NO_LPM },
+
 	/* Realtek Semiconductor Corp. Mass Storage Device (Multicard Reader)*/
 	{ USB_DEVICE(0x0bda, 0x0151), .driver_info = USB_QUIRK_CONFIG_INTF_STRINGS },
 
--
2.49.0
